'.agent_tasks/four-lenses-agents-working_memory/README.md', 'content': '# Four 
Lenses Bug Report: src/henchman/agents/working_memory.py\n## Date: 
2025-03-16T20:00:00Z\n## Bug Summary\nThe `_validate_and_normalize_line_numbers`
method incorrectly handles boolean values passed as line numbers due to using 
`type(start) is not int` instead of `not isinstance(start, int)`, which rejects 
`bool` values (True/False) that are subclasses of `int` in Python.\n\n## 
Lens\n**Lens 1: Logic Gaps** - The code assumes only `int` values will be 
passed, but Python\'s type system allows `bool` values (True/False) which are 
technically subclasses of `int`. This creates a silent failure where `True` 
(which equals 1) or `False` (which equals 0) would be incorrectly 
rejected.\n\n## Expected vs. Observed\n| Expected Behavior | Observed Risk 
|\n|---|---|\n| Boolean values True/False should be treated as integers (True=1,
False=0) for line number validation | Boolean values are rejected with "Invalid 
line number: start must be an integer, got bool=True" |\n| Line number 1 should 
be accepted whether passed as `1` or `True` | `True` is rejected even though it 
equals 1 |\n| Line number 0 should be rejected whether passed as `0` or `False` 
| `False` is rejected with confusing error message |\n\n## Root Cause\nPython\'s
`bool` type is a subclass of `int` (`issubclass(bool, int)` returns `True`). The
code uses `type(start) is not int` which checks for exact type equality, not 
subclass relationship. When a boolean value is passed (e.g., from a conditional 
expression), it fails validation even though `True == 1` and `False == 0`.\n\n##
Fix Plan\nChange `type(start) is not int` to `not isinstance(start, int)` in the
`_validate_and_normalize_line_numbers` method to properly handle boolean values 
as integers. This will allow `True` and `False` to be validated as line numbers 
(True=1, False=0).\n\n## Status: IN PROGRESS'})
